    "case_id": "618",
    "case_summary": "A 70-year-old man with a medical history of atrial fibrillation, sick sinus syndrome with pacemaker placement, prostate cancer treated with radiation and hormonal therapy, obstructive sleep apnea, left-sided Bell's palsy, and tobacco misuse presented with 3 weeks of right-sided temporal headache and 3 days of diplopia.\n\nPhysical examination revealed:- Preexisting left-sided incomplete facial nerve palsy- Right cranial nerve VI and III palsies- Visual acuity and peripheral vision intact bilaterally- No miosis, mydriasis, conjunctival injection, or lacrimation- Severe photosensitivity- Normal funduscopic examination- No other neurologic deficits\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Initial complete blood count: unremarkable- Comprehensive metabolic panel: unremarkable- Erythrocyte sedimentation rate: unremarkable- C-reactive protein: unremarkable- Thyroid-stimulating hormone: unremarkable- CSF microbiology: negative- CSF cytology: no malignant cellsImaging Studies:1. CTA of head and neck:   - Air fluid levels in bilateral sphenoid and maxillary sinuses   - Otherwise unremarkable2. MRI of brain and orbits with/without contrast:   Image Title: Axial and coronal T1 MRI with contrast pretreatment   Image Description: Avid irregular enhancement within the right cavernous sinus and enlargement of the right side compared with the left3. CT thorax findings:   - Left pleural effusion   - Mediastinal lymphadenopathy   - Mass-like thickening of the posterior left pleura4. CT abdomen and pelvis:   - Mesenteric lymphadenopathyPathology:- Biopsy of posterior left pleural thickening showed CD5+, CD10−, non-germinal center B-cell type- Fluorescence in situ hybridization analysis: negative for Myc, BCL6, t(14;18)/IGH-BCL2, and t(11;14)/CCND1-IGH gene rearrangements- Bone marrow biopsy: normal",
